WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.344
Most CSS tutorials tell you what a custom property is.

2
00:00:03.424 --> 00:00:05.676
AI Tutor makes you prove what it does.

3
00:00:05.756 --> 00:00:08.470
In the next five minutes, a student will import a

4
00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:11.591
real page, ask the Tutor a question, make a prediction,

5
00:00:11.591 --> 00:00:14.033
change one global color token, and compare

6
00:00:14.033 --> 00:00:15.729
the result with the original.

7
00:00:15.809 --> 00:00:18.846
Everything you see is running in the real product, inside

8
00:00:18.846 --> 00:00:20.174
a fresh browser session.

9
00:00:20.354 --> 00:00:23.231
We begin with two ordinary files: index

10
00:00:23.231 --> 00:00:25.149
dot HTML and theme dot CSS.

11
00:00:25.229 --> 00:00:29.485
AI Tutor normalizes the upload, removes executable scripts, and renders

12
00:00:29.485 --> 00:00:31.475
the page inside a sandboxed frame.

13
00:00:31.555 --> 00:00:34.316
The exercise is deliberately small, but it is not a

14
00:00:34.316 --> 00:00:35.631
screenshot or a mockup.

15
00:00:35.711 --> 00:00:40.390
The button, cards, borders, labels, and code highlights are all

16
00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:41.690
live HTML and CSS.

17
00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:43.698
The interface and lesson are presented in

18
00:00:43.698 --> 00:00:45.020
English for this recording.

19
00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:47.181
The purple theme is controlled by custom

20
00:00:47.181 --> 00:00:48.811
properties in the root selector.

21
00:00:48.891 --> 00:00:52.728
Names such as brand, surface, text, accent, and line describe

22
00:00:52.728 --> 00:00:55.753
design roles instead of individual components.

23
00:00:55.833 --> 00:00:59.467
That is the first useful pattern: choose semantic token names.

24
00:00:59.547 --> 00:01:02.314
A token called brand can serve a button, an eyebrow

25
00:01:02.314 --> 00:01:04.423
label, a border, and a code highlight.

26
00:01:04.503 --> 00:01:07.035
A name such as purple button would lock the variable

27
00:01:07.035 --> 00:01:08.566
to one color and one component.

28
00:01:08.746 --> 00:01:10.720
Now we open the actual source.

29
00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.041
Root gives these tokens page-wide scope.

30
00:01:13.121 --> 00:01:17.175
Custom properties participate in the cascade, and descendants inherit them

31
00:01:17.175 --> 00:01:18.984
unless a closer declaration wins.

32
00:01:19.064 --> 00:01:22.008
Components consume a token with the var function, while an

33
00:01:22.008 --> 00:01:24.532
optional second argument can provide a fallback.

34
00:01:24.612 --> 00:01:27.552
The code highlight also uses color mix, deriving a softer

35
00:01:27.552 --> 00:01:31.044
tint from brand instead of introducing another unrelated literal.

36
00:01:31.124 --> 00:01:34.978
So the page already exposes a small design system: centralized

37
00:01:34.978 --> 00:01:38.322
decisions at the top, reusable component rules below.

38
00:01:38.502 --> 00:01:41.689
Before editing, the student opens the AI Tutor and asks

39
00:01:41.689 --> 00:01:45.569
a specific question: if brand changes, which visible parts should

40
00:01:45.569 --> 00:01:46.954
move together, and why?

41
00:01:47.034 --> 00:01:49.275
The Tutor does not need to take over the keyboard.

42
00:01:49.355 --> 00:01:52.627
It checks the current canvas and source, responds in English,

43
00:01:52.627 --> 00:01:54.893
and asks the learner to predict the effect.

44
00:01:54.973 --> 00:01:58.140
The student answers that every rule reading var brand should

45
00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:01.370
update, while text and surface tokens should stay unchanged.

46
00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:04.966
This interaction matters: the AI is guiding an observable claim,

47
00:02:04.966 --> 00:02:06.883
not merely producing a definition.

48
00:02:07.063 --> 00:02:10.684
With that prediction recorded, we change only brand, from purple

49
00:02:10.684 --> 00:02:12.659
to deep teal, and run a safe preview.

50
00:02:12.739 --> 00:02:15.913
Watch the same value propagate to the primary button, the

51
00:02:15.913 --> 00:02:18.227
eyebrow label, the hero border, the token

52
00:02:18.227 --> 00:02:19.880
board, and the code emphasis.

53
00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:21.678
The HTML has not changed.

54
00:02:21.758 --> 00:02:23.929
The component selectors have not changed.

55
00:02:24.009 --> 00:02:27.266
One decision moved through every consumer because each rule points

56
00:02:27.266 --> 00:02:28.351
back to the same token.

57
00:02:28.431 --> 00:02:31.020
That is the cause-and-effect link the learner predicted.

58
00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:34.133
The Tutor can now ground the explanation in the visible

59
00:02:34.133 --> 00:02:37.704
result: the edited value, the affected target, the matching rule,

60
00:02:37.704 --> 00:02:39.106
and the current revision.

61
00:02:39.186 --> 00:02:41.375
If those facts do not line up, the product is

62
00:02:41.375 --> 00:02:43.442
designed to say that evidence is missing

63
00:02:43.442 --> 00:02:45.874
instead of inventing a confident explanation.

64
00:02:45.954 --> 00:02:47.719
The student still performs the edit.

65
00:02:47.799 --> 00:02:51.337
AI provides a question, a bounded inspection, and feedback; it

66
00:02:51.337 --> 00:02:53.940
does not silently complete the learning task.

67
00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:56.152
Next we build a complete mint palette.

68
00:02:56.232 --> 00:03:00.746
Brand soft, page surface, card surface, primary text, muted text,

69
00:03:00.746 --> 00:03:04.614
accent, and border tokens change in one compact section.

70
00:03:04.694 --> 00:03:07.556
Notice what stays stable: the HTML structure,

71
00:03:07.556 --> 00:03:09.977
layout, spacing, and component rules.

72
00:03:10.057 --> 00:03:13.076
This is why global variables scale beyond a single trick.

73
00:03:13.156 --> 00:03:17.616
They separate design decisions from component implementation, make themes easier

74
00:03:17.616 --> 00:03:19.249
to audit, and reduce the risk of

75
00:03:19.249 --> 00:03:21.259
leaving one hard-coded color behind.

76
00:03:21.439 --> 00:03:25.096
The editor distinguishes a transient preview from a committed version.

77
00:03:25.176 --> 00:03:26.779
We run the candidate first.

78
00:03:26.859 --> 00:03:28.696
If the CSS is invalid or unsafe,

79
00:03:28.696 --> 00:03:31.735
the last known-good rendering remains available.

80
00:03:31.815 --> 00:03:34.690
When the preview succeeds, we give it a meaningful name

81
00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:36.378
and save an immutable revision.

82
00:03:36.458 --> 00:03:38.896
The original purple page is not overwritten.

83
00:03:38.976 --> 00:03:43.222
That makes experimentation recoverable: a learner can try, inspect, fail

84
00:03:43.222 --> 00:03:45.648
safely, correct the code, and still return

85
00:03:45.648 --> 00:03:46.592
to a known state.

86
00:03:46.772 --> 00:03:50.580
Finally, AI Tutor places the original and mint versions together.

87
00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:54.229
The comparison turns a visual impression into an explanation: which

88
00:03:54.229 --> 00:03:58.659
token changed, which consumers responded, and which properties remained constant.

89
00:03:58.739 --> 00:04:01.066
The same evidence model supports lessons on

90
00:04:01.066 --> 00:04:03.267
the box model, Flexbox, and positioning.

91
00:04:03.347 --> 00:04:07.381
Versioned Learning Proof can replay student steps, Tutor messages, tool

92
00:04:07.381 --> 00:04:10.895
results, fact receipts, and canvas saves on one timeline, while

93
00:04:10.895 --> 00:04:14.539
keeping machine evidence separate from human learning outcomes.

94
00:04:14.719 --> 00:04:17.656
This recording shows implemented product behavior.

95
00:04:17.736 --> 00:04:20.148
It does not claim that a learner study has already

96
00:04:20.148 --> 00:04:22.972
proved better outcomes; that research is still pending.

97
00:04:23.052 --> 00:04:26.958
Realtime Tutor also depends on an authenticated local Codex service,

98
00:04:26.958 --> 00:04:29.937
while deterministic lessons remain usable offline.

99
00:04:30.017 --> 00:04:33.322
The core idea is straightforward: let the student predict, let

100
00:04:33.322 --> 00:04:36.004
the page reveal the effect, let the Tutor ground the

101
00:04:36.004 --> 00:04:38.685
explanation, and then preserve enough evidence to

102
00:04:38.685 --> 00:04:40.431
revisit what actually happened.

103
00:04:40.511 --> 00:04:42.717
Change once, understand everywhere.
